ng school closures  turkey's education and science workers  union warns turkish pupils are falling  behind  online lessons are not very effective we  have basically lost an entire year of  teaching  we never understood why schools  supposedly pose a greater infection risk  than cafes or restaurants  turkish school closures have hit poor  families like the aslan's  hardest some four million turkish school  children don't have internet access at  home  that means mohammed azlan must rely on  the educational programs on turkish  state television  people with money can send their  children to private schools and pay  tutors to help their children catch up  we can't afford that  our children have to resort to  educational television shows  wealthier parents like zhandan gunveren  are finding turkey's economic situation  increasingly difficult too jandan says  more and more parents are taking their  youngest  out of her kindergarten if the trend  continues  she may go out of business but  matters are even worse for turkey's  school children  they are the ones paying the highest  price in this pandemic  lanka is assistant professor of social  policy at the belgian university k.u  lurvin  so can we expect all these children just  to catch up  once the pandemic's really over or are  we looking at long-term  effects for example job prospects  well i have to say i'm afraid we are  looking really at these long-term  effects  in terms of educational attainment in  terms of job prospects  and if we don't try to mitigate the  impact of these school closures on the  most vulnerable children and students  i'm afraid we'll be jeopardizing their  future it's kind of well known  that educational attainment and in  particular your higher education  attainment  is really is really important for your  later job prospects  and so if those children don't have this  higher education attainment  you know they will have trouble finding  decent jobs  later on in their life and this might  reinforce this cycle of inequality that  we're witnessing  just how many children are we actually  talking about how many schools  were closed uh during the pandemic  well you know at the peak of the first  wave in april 2020 we'll be talking  about  one and a half billion children  worldwide who have been affected  by these uh by these school closures but  even today after a second wave and  sometimes a third wave  you know at the global scale we we still  see that over 200 million children are  affected and that their  schooling experience their learning has  been interrupted  and even today also in europe in  countries such as germany or belgium and  norway for instance  schools are still often partially closed  and many students still have to deal  with remote learning  so it affects a lot a lot of children on  a global scale  absolutely when we've been talking about  learning and  also the fact that these kids have been  having a hard time and having to catch  up but  what about something as important as  social skills that uh kids also learn  at school which are so important later  on in life  yes i mean i mean i mean i mean the  schooling outcomes are very important of  course but schools are more than that as  we all know right  also social places social environments  places where  children meet other children but  children play where children sort of  you know also can eat healthy for  instance that is one important function  of a school besides learning and  education and we know that school  closures have had detrimental effects on  that  on on that part as well on that account  as well and of course  affecting those most vulnerable children  those most disadvantaged children who  you  you know who stand to gain the most from  having  social contacts with other children so  this is absolutely a problem as well  besides you know  the learning outcome issue so give me  some  solutions what are we going to do about  this can can we change  the situation  well um 